WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.240
When you first start building a React Native app, throwing everything into one big components folder works fine.

2
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.560
But as your project grows, that flat structure quickly turns into a tangled mess of overlapping dependencies.

3
00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:15.520
Think about the last time a new developer joined your team.

4
00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:20.800
If they have to spend their first three hours just trying to locate the basic code for the login screen,

5
00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:23.920
your underlying architecture is simply slowing you down.

6
00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:26.640
Let's fix that using a standard sample app.

7
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.080
As you can see, this application has three distinct areas,

8
00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:33.920
a login flow, a main scrolling feed, and a user profile.

9
00:00:33.920 --> 00:00:38.240
Our goal is to reorganize this specific app into a feature-based architecture.

10
00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.320
By the end, we will map out these exact boundaries and lock them in place with an architecture.md document.

11
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:47.520
Step 1. Open your IDE and start your project.

12
00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:51.120
Keep your iOS or Android simulator running right next to your code,

13
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:54.640
so we can verify the app stays stable while we move things around.

14
00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:02.000
Moving from a flat list of files to a structured architecture means developers know exactly where to look for a bug.

15
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.240
It means new hires can contribute on day one instead of day five.

16
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.400
Let's build the new skeleton.

17
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.560
Create three root folders, app, features, and shared.

18
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.640
Think of them like parts of a house.

19
00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.080
App is the mechanical room, powering core systems.

20
00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:20.880
Features holds individual, isolated rooms,

21
00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.640
and shared contains the base building blocks used everywhere.

22
00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:26.960
Now, open that features folder.

23
00:01:26.960 --> 00:01:31.280
Inside it, create three separate subfolders matching our application domains,

24
00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:33.840
off, feed, and profile.

25
00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:36.720
Go through your code base and start moving the files.

26
00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:41.040
Take the login screen, its specific hooks, and the authentication API calls,

27
00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.080
and drop them entirely inside the auth folder.

28
00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.600
Do the exact same thing for the feed and profile domains.

29
00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:52.880
As you do this, your terminal is going to light up with red arrow messages about broken imports.

30
00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:59.040
Use your IDE's auto update paths feature to let it fix those broken links for you automatically.

31
00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:01.040
Look over at your running simulator.

32
00:02:01.040 --> 00:02:06.240
Force a reload and verify that the login screen still routes correctly to the main feed.

33
00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:10.160
By locking domain specific files inside their own feature folders,

34
00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:16.640
you guarantee that a developer working on the user profile won't accidentally write code that breaks the main feed.

35
00:02:16.640 --> 00:02:20.320
Your main navigation setups, like stack or tab navigators,

36
00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.720
sit directly inside the app folder, routing users into feature blocks.

37
00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:26.720
The same applies to global state.

38
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:30.400
Stores like SueStand or Redux hold application-wide data,

39
00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.960
so they also live in the app directory.

40
00:02:32.960 --> 00:02:35.520
Now, shift in retention to the shared directory.

41
00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.240
This is where you extract generic UI elements that don't belong to any specific domain,

42
00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:42.800
like your standard buttons or typography.

43
00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:46.720
The rule of thumb for whether an asset belongs here is straightforward.

44
00:02:46.720 --> 00:02:50.320
If you can drop a file into a completely different reactinative app,

45
00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.760
and it still works without changes, it is truly shared.

46
00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.320
A generic submit button easily passes,

47
00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:00.160
but a specialized feed card fails, staying locked inside the feed folder.

48
00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:05.680
Rigidly enforcing the separation prevents the shared folder from turning into a junk drawer.

49
00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.280
It forces your domain specific logic to stay neatly isolated.

50
00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.280
To finalize the structure, go to the root of your project directory

51
00:03:13.280 --> 00:03:16.320
and create a new file named architecture.md.

52
00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:20.080
Start by writing down exactly where screens belong.

53
00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:25.680
Specify that a screen view must always reside inside the screen sub-director of its respective feature folder.

54
00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:29.040
Next, define the clear rules for hooks.

55
00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.760
Generic utility hooks go in the shared directory,

56
00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:35.040
while domain specific hooks stay local to their feature folder.

57
00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.320
Add a section defining where API services live

58
00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:42.640
and explicitly state that all custom iOS or Android modules

59
00:03:42.640 --> 00:03:45.520
must be kept isolated in the root native folder.

60
00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:51.440
Writing these decisions down transforms an implicit structure into an explicit map.

61
00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:56.240
Every pull request that gets reviewed from now on can be checked against the standard.

62
00:03:56.800 --> 00:04:00.640
We've moved from a massive, unsorted list of files on the left

63
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.000
to a highly organized, logical directory structure on the right.

64
00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:06.400
Check your simulator one last time.

65
00:04:06.880 --> 00:04:09.840
Despite moving nearly every file in the project,

66
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.200
the user interface functions exactly as it did when we started.

67
00:04:13.760 --> 00:04:19.200
Commit your new architecture and distribute the architecture.md file to your development team

68
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:22.240
so everyone starts working from the same blueprint immediately.

69
00:04:22.800 --> 00:04:26.080
If you want to dig deeper into the theory behind this organization,

70
00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:30.080
search for the guide titled Feature Based folder structure in React Native

71
00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.480
to see how large teams implement these patterns.

72
00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.160
Your code base is now structurally prepared.

73
00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.600
You can add 20 new features and 10 new developers,

74
00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:43.760
and the project will remain predictable and easy to maintain.

